{"id": "sevt_01BQJbeUxDwnvbRaHgFdnaxH", "content": [{"text": "Checkout p99 went from about 75 ms to about 3.4 s at 14:22, and the checkout deploy that caused it landed at 14:22:30.\n\n- **Deploys:** checkout `6db1b9b` went out at 14:22:30. The cart deploy `50293ce` (14:21:05) was the other candidate. Auth `67f6130` was at 11:58, far too early to matter.\n- **Metric alignment:** p99 was flat at 66\u201380 ms until 14:21 and jumped to 3404 ms in the 14:22 sample. It then kept climbing to about 5.8 s by 14:59. The 14:22 sample is the first bad one, so the exact gap to the 14:22:30 deploy is within a minute. The deploy and spike are essentially simultaneous.\n- **Diff `6db1b9b`:** the \"readability refactor\" replaced one batched query (`order_id IN (...)`) plus in-memory grouping with a query per order row. That is an N+1 query pattern.\n- **Log confirmation:** before the deploy, `app.log` shows single `order_id IN (?)` queries per request. About 3,800 per-row `order_id = ?` queries appear after it.\n- **Cart `50293ce`:** it only adds backoff sleep to retries. That only slows requests when the pricing client is already failing, and nothing here points to that. I read it as a red herring.\n\nI only counted and sampled the log. I did not check `db_pool_utilization` or cart's own latency.\n\n**Root cause:** `6db1b9b` \u2014 the checkout summary refactor replaced one batched `order_items` query with one query per order row (N+1), so latency grows with the number of open orders. Reverting it should restore the batched query.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:42:20.054526Z", "type": "agent.message"}
